<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0"><head><title>Triple-O in Pointliner</title></head><body>
<outline text="# Triple-O in Pointliner">
  <outline text="Triple-O is a player character emulator: you sit in the GM seat and the dice tell you what your party does. This demo wires the whole tool into pills. Every one of them is live, so click any pill to run it. All thirteen of the zine's random tables ship complete below, because Triple-O is openly licensed. See triple-o.md for the walkthrough."/>
  <outline text="Adapted from Triple-O: The Player Character Emulator (version 1.0.2) by Cezar Capacle, used under CC BY-SA 4.0 (creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0). Changes made: the procedures and tables are restructured into outline points and pills, and the d66 tables are rewritten as flat thirty-six-option picks. Because Triple-O is ShareAlike, the adapted Triple-O material here is likewise CC BY-SA 4.0. That is separate from Pointliner's own AGPLv3 licence, which covers the app and not this game content. Triple-O is named descriptively; no logo or art is reproduced.">
    <outline text="The tables here carry the zine's own entries, not filler. Support the designer: capacle.itch.io/triple-o-zine."/>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## The Triple-O Check">
    <outline text="On paper the Check is 1d6 read against a key: 4-6 is the Obvious, 2-3 the Option, 1 the Odd. A weighted pick spells the same odds directly, so the pill answers in the words of the game instead of handing you a number to look up. The weights are how many of the six faces each band owns."/>
    <outline text="{rule tripleo: the Obvious 3 | the Option 2 | the Odd 1}"/>
    <outline text="The name has no hyphen on purpose. A rule name is a plain identifier, so `triple-o` would register nothing and every call to it would sit on the page as literal characters."/>
    <outline text="The wizard finds a grimoire. Obvious: she opens it at once. Option: detect magic first, then pocket it. Odd: left blank until the die asks for it. She goes with {tripleo}."/>
    <outline text="Leaving the Odd blank is the zine's own speed trick, and it works better here than on paper: roll first, then write only the branch you actually got."/>
    <outline text="Double-Down">
      <outline text="When a character is leaning a certain way, roll two dice and keep whichever result feels right. This one stays raw dice rather than two `{tripleo}` pills, because the doubles rule reads the faces: {1d6} and {1d6}."/>
      <outline text="Read each die against the key (4-6 Obvious, 2-3 Option, 1 Odd) and keep your favourite. Two matching faces means the behaviour sticks: mark that Trait PREVALENT on the sheet below, or write a new one for what you just learned."/>
      <outline text="Two `{tripleo}` pills would be the wrong tool here, and the reason is worth knowing. A band pill has already thrown the die away, so &quot;both pills agree&quot; is not the same event as doubles: bands match about two times in five, doubles one in six."/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="The Check as a resolution system">
      <outline text="Same pill, different question. Instead of three things the character might do, write three things that might happen, then roll: {tripleo}. Nothing new to learn, which is the point."/>
      <outline text="The warrior is ambushed by a giant spider. Obvious: he fights it off but takes a bite. Option: he deflects clean and strikes back. Odd: it overwhelms him before he can raise the sword. The result is {tripleo}."/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Group actions">
    <outline text="When the party decides together, put up three courses of action. If one is clearly dominant, make it the Obvious and roll {tripleo} as usual."/>
    <outline text="If no course dominates, let them argue: roll a die for each and rank them. Camp at the gate {1d6}, push inside and rest there {1d6}, split up {1d6}. Highest is the Obvious, middle the Option, lowest the Odd. Then roll {tripleo} to see which one carries."/>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## The party you are emulating">
    <outline text="Three characters, three to five Traits each, written as short snippets. Each Trait is its own point carrying one label, and the character name above them carries theirs. A label reaches everything below it, so a Trait belongs to both without either label being typed twice."/>
    <outline text="One label per point is not fussiness, it is what the drawn text reads like. A roll strips the label it matched and leaves every other one showing, so a Trait wearing two labels comes back out of the oracle with a stray one still attached."/>
    <outline text="Bran, the warrior #bran">
      <outline text="Battlemaster, stocky, late thirties #trait"/>
      <outline text="Charismatic leader #trait"/>
      <outline text="Grew up on a farm #trait"/>
      <outline text="Driven by legacy #trait"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="Sella, the thief #sella">
      <outline text="Chaotic, allergic to a plan #trait"/>
      <outline text="Deeply indebted to someone dangerous #trait"/>
      <outline text="Curious about ruins #trait"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="Nim, the wizard #nim">
      <outline text="Arcane scholar, reads first and asks later #trait"/>
      <outline text="Seeks a forgotten spellbook #trait"/>
      <outline text="Magically drained, still recovering #trait"/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## The Trait Sheet">
    <outline text="The zine's sheet has three status boxes: a Trait is DEFAULT, or PREVALENT once it has proven itself, or TEMPORARY when it is a condition you picked up. The base below is that sheet. It opens on its Board view, one lane per status, so a Double-Down that rolls doubles becomes a gesture: drag the card from DEFAULT to PREVALENT and the sheet records that the character changed."/>
    <outline text="Lanes exist because the statuses are declared as a state set by the pill below. An undeclared value would land its card in a &quot;No state&quot; lane instead. RETIRED is this guide's addition, not the zine's: a sequence needs at least one finished state right of the bar, and it is where a condition goes once it has healed or hardened into a Default."/>
    <outline text="{seq Trait: DEFAULT PREVALENT TEMPORARY | RETIRED}"/>
    <outline _type="base" _colrole="[null,&quot;status&quot;,null,null]" _view="{&quot;kind&quot;:&quot;board&quot;,&quot;groupBy&quot;:1}" text="| Character | Status | Trait | Category |&#10;|---|---|---|---|&#10;| Bran | DEFAULT | Charismatic leader | PR |&#10;| Bran | PREVALENT | Grew up on a farm | BG |&#10;| Bran | TEMPORARY | Injured forearm | CN |&#10;| Sella | DEFAULT | Deeply indebted | BG |&#10;| Sella | DEFAULT | Chaotic | PR |&#10;| Nim | DEFAULT | Seeks a forgotten spellbook | MT |&#10;| Nim | TEMPORARY | Magically drained | CN |"/>
    <outline text="Category is the zine's shorthand for where a Trait came from: CL class, PR personality, SK skill, BG background, MT motivation, CN condition. Switch the base to Table at its top left to edit cells, or to Cards."/>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Roll on your own party">
    <outline text="This is the part no printed table can do. A roll pill takes an ordinary search and returns one random point that matches it, so the party above IS a table. Add a Trait to a character and the next roll can land on it, with no setup."/>
    <outline text="Whose Trait decides this moment? {roll: #trait}"/>
    <outline text="What surfaces in Bran? {roll: #trait #bran}"/>
    <outline text="Freeze one for the whole scene: tonight the scene turns on {pivot := {roll: #trait}}. Later in the same scene, {pivot} is still the same Trait. Click the frozen pill to draw a new one."/>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Spark tables">
    <outline text="Six tables of abstract words to interpret in context. On paper each is a d66: roll two dice and read the digits as a pair, which gives thirty-six even outcomes. In Pointliner a d66 is not a die at all, it is a flat thirty-six-option pick, and that is exactly what each rule below is. Do not reach for a sixty-six-sided die here: it would roll 1 to 66 evenly, which is a different table with thirty extra faces that no entry sits on."/>
    <outline text="Each rule is declared once and then callable by name anywhere in the document, as many times as you like."/>
    <outline text="The six tables">
      <outline text="{rule action: Abort | Advance | Aim | Block | Break | Challenge | Climb | Collide | Combat | Concentrate | Consolidate | Coordinate | Cover | Deceive | Deflect | Delve | Destroy | Dodge | Endure | Force | Grab | Help | Impact | Intensify | Invest | Knock down | Lose | Oppose | Penetrate | Persevere | Probe | Protect | Retreat | Separate | Stun | Surprise}"/>
      <outline text="{rule focus: Advantage | Ally | Balance | Barrier | Chance | Control | Courage | Damage | Defense | Determination | Environment | Fear | Ferocity | Focus | Impulse | Instinct | Maneuver | Object | Opening | Position | Posture | Power | Precision | Pride | Reach | Resource | Senses | Speed | Strategy | Strength | Subtlety | Technique | Weakness | Weapon | Wits | Wound}"/>
      <outline text="{rule method: Adaptation | Agility | Camouflage | Coercion | Cunning | Deception | Distraction | Fear | Guile | Imitation | Improvisation | Ingenuity | Instinct | Intimidation | Knowledge | Magic | Manipulation | Observation | Patience | Persistence | Resilience | Sacrifice | Scheming | Sorcery | Speed | Stealth | Strategy | Strength | Subterfuge | Surprise | Technology | Tenacity | Terrain | Trickery | Violence | Weapons}"/>
      <outline text="{rule disposition: Aggressive | Aloof | Ambivalent | Antagonistic | Bold | Cautious | Cheerful | Confident | Cooperative | Curious | Defensive | Detached | Dismissive | Distrustful | Eager | Enthusiastic | Friendly | Guarded | Helpful | Hostile | Indifferent | Inquisitive | Interested | Inviting | Open | Optimistic | Pessimistic | Reluctant | Resentful | Respectful | Suspicious | Sympathetic | Timid | Uninterested | Warm | Wary}"/>
      <outline text="{rule motivation: Ambition | Anger | Atonement | Betrayal | Challenge | Curiosity | Despair | Duty | Empathy | Envy | Faith | Fear | Greed | Guilt | Hate | Honor | Hope | Ideology | Justice | Knowledge | Legacy | Love | Loyalty | Mastery | Obsession | Passion | Pride | Regret | Rivalry | Sacrifice | Shame | Sorrow | Survival | Thrill | Tradition | Vengeance}"/>
      <outline text="{rule dynamics: Admiring | Affectionate | Amused | Comfortable | Competitive | Conflicted | Contemptuous | Deferential | Defiant | Dependent | Disappointed | Distant | Envious | Exasperated | Fascinated | Grateful | Guilty | Indifferent | Inspired | Intimidated | Intrigued | Judgmental | Loyal | Observant | Patronizing | Protective | Resentful | Respectful | Skeptical | Supportive | Suspicious | Sympathetic | Teasing | Threatened | Trusting | Uneasy}"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="Rolling them">
      <outline text="One word at a time: an Action is {action}, a Focus is {focus}, a Method is {method}, a Disposition is {disposition}, a Motivation is {motivation}, and how one character feels about another is {dynamics}."/>
      <outline text="The zine's workhorse combos, each a rule of its own so you click once instead of twice."/>
      <outline text="{rule actionfocus: {action} + {focus}}"/>
      <outline text="{rule actionmethod: {action} + {method}}"/>
      <outline text="{rule actionmotivation: {action} + {motivation}}"/>
      <outline text="What they do and what they are watching: {actionfocus}. What they do and how: {actionmethod}. What they do and why: {actionmotivation}."/>
      <outline text="{rule npcsketch: {disposition}, driven by {motivation}, works by {method}, and right now they {action}}"/>
      <outline text="An unwritten innkeeper, in one click: {npcsketch}"/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Specific tables">
    <outline text="Seven tables of concrete moves for the situations most adventures keep returning to. These need no interpreting: read the result and play it. If one does not fit the character, roll again."/>
    <outline text="The seven tables">
      <outline text="{rule combat: Aim carefully, wait for an opening | Attack directly with primary weapon | Attack with secondary weapon | Attempt to disarm the enemy | Attempt to flee | Call for a combined action | Call for parley | Cast a spell or use a special ability | Change elevation (climb, jump) | Charge recklessly | Create difficult terrain or obstacles | Destroy enemy cover or resources | Distract or mislead | Draw attention/taunt the enemy | Drop your weapons/surrender | Fall back and regroup | Flank or reposition | Force movement (push, pull, reposition) | Grapple or restrain an opponent | Heal, buff, or protect an ally | Help or assist an ally's action | Hold position and guard | Improvise a weapon | Look for a weakness | Make a feint or fake attack | Rally/inspire to boost morale | Ready an action to respond to a trigger | Set up an ambush or trap | Switch weapons or tactics | Take cover or duck behind something | Target a leader or key figure | Try to intimidate or scare them off | Use a consumable item | Use environment to their advantage | Withdraw or disengage safely | Yell a warning or call for help}"/>
      <outline text="{rule social: Appeal to emotion | Appeal to logic | Appeal to rules, laws, or customs | Appeal to their values or beliefs | Apply leverage/blackmail | Ask probing questions | Bluff or exaggerate | Bribe or offer incentive | Challenge to a duel, contest, or wager | Compliment or flatter someone | Confide a secret | Directly inquire about a piece of information | Empathize with their situation or feelings | Exploit rivalries or conflicting interests | Give a gift or token of goodwill | Haggle over price or terms | Invoke shared values, traditions, or history | Invoke status, rank, or reputation | Make a concession to move talks | Misdirect or change the subject | Name-drop important connections | Offer a trade or bargain | Offer help or assistance | Perform/entertain to win favor | Pretend to agree | Promise something uncertain | Read the room for mood and dynamics | Set and enforce boundaries | Share a personal story or vulnerability | Speak honestly and openly | Stay silent and observe | Tell a joke or lighten the mood | Try to charm or impress | Try to find common ground | Try to read their intentions | Use threats or a show of force}"/>
      <outline text="{rule exploration: Assess the terrain ahead | Check for traps or hazards | Climb or seek vantage point | Collect specimens or samples | Cover tracks | Document discoveries | Forage for food, water, or supplies | Hold back and observe | Hunt or fish | Leave messages or signs | Listen for echoes/water/wind/animals | Look for magical or strange phenomena | Look for shelter from the elements | Look for signs of civilization | Look for tracks or signs of passage | Map or mark the area | Navigate by stars/sun/landmarks | Observe wildlife patterns for clues | Produce source of light or heat | Propose a change in marching order or pace | Propose a short rest | Rush to cover more ground | Scout ahead carefully | Search for hidden paths | Send a companion or summoned being ahead | Stand guard or keep watch | Stay alert and move slowly | Study the weather | Suggest to press on for longer | Suggest to split up the group | Take a risk to save time | Test the air or water | Test the ground or depth | Try a different route | Try to identify a plant, creature, or landmark | Use a specialized instrument}"/>
      <outline text="{rule delving: Adjust formation or spacing | Backtrack to a previous junction | Check for pressure plates or tripwires | Check for recent signs of passage or presence | Check the ceiling for hazards or instability | Climb or take the high ground | Collect samples | Cover tracks or conceal presence | Create a distraction, lure, or decoy | Document findings and warnings | Examine remains or debris for clues | Examine the structure for weaknesses | Feel for air currents or temperature changes | Follow an unexplored route | Hide in the shadows or a crawlspace | Listen at a door, wall, or passage | Look for creature tracks, territorial signs, or lair markings | Map the layout and connections | Mark your path to avoid going in circles | Peek into a room using a mirror or tool | Probe the floor or walls with a pole | Push recklessly onward | Rally the group for a breather | Scout ahead of the party | Seal, block, or barricade an entry | Search for hidden doors, passages, or mechanisms | Search for hidden loot or cached supplies | Search for inscriptions, carvings, or symbols | Send a familiar or summoned creature ahead first | Send a light source ahead | Sense for magical residues | Set tripwires, alarms, or warning markers | Sniff the air for gas, smoke, rot, or chemicals | Study architecture for purpose, origin, logic, or age | Tend to wounds, fatigue, or morale | Toss something ahead to test a room}"/>
      <outline text="{rule interpretation: Believe it's a trap or test | Believe they are being lied to or deceived | Connect it to a larger pattern | Dismiss it as a distraction or misdirection | Feel a strong sense of personal duty or responsibility | Feel distrust or suspicion | Feel intense, unfiltered curiosity or fascination | Feel nostalgia or déjà vu | Feel overwhelmed | Get a strong gut feeling that something is wrong | Interpret it as a symbolic message or warning | Misinterpret the situation entirely | Misjudge the intentions of others | Obsess over one detail | Overanalyze every detail | Rationalize it away | Read it as a bad omen or a dark portent | Recognize something as valuable or useful | See connections to previous events | See hidden danger or threat | See it as a challenge or provocation | Seize it as the perfect chance to act | Sense opportunity or advantage | Suspect a hidden reward or secret is nearby | Suspect an ambush | Take it as an invitation or welcome | Take it at face value | Take it personally | Think it's a coincidence | Think it's a performance or an act | Treat it as a key clue to a larger mystery | Treat it as fate or destiny | Trust intuition over logic | Try to read between the lines | View it as a puzzle that needs to be solved | View it as an audition or chance to impress}"/>
      <outline text="{rule downtime: Admit a fear or weakness | Ask an ally for advice on a personal dilemma | Ask someone about their motives | Compare beliefs or philosophies | Confess a secret, a lie, or a hidden motive | Confide a lingering doubt | Cook, share food, or offer a drink with intention | Debate a moral choice | Discuss long-term dreams for when the journey ends | Express admiration or gratitude | Express homesickness or longing | Gamble or play a game of chance or skill | Grieve something lost | Indulge in a personal coping mechanism or vice | Inquire about an ally's homeland or family | Offer comfort or encouragement | Ponder about the true nature of their enemies | Practice a skill or hobby | Pray, meditate, or observe a personal tradition | Propose a solemn pact | Question the group's direction or purpose | Rally the group with a speech or toast | Share a cultural ritual, song, or saying | Share a keepsake or memento and its story | Share a story from their childhood | Share rumors or speculation | Sing, play music, or perform for the group | Suggest a change in plans | Talk about a dream you once had | Talk about someone you left behind | Tease, joke, or try to lighten the mood | Tell a story of failure or glory | Tend to wounds or worn gear | Try to teach an ally a minor skill, trick, or phrase | Wander off alone into the dark | Write, sketch, or record thoughts}"/>
      <outline text="{rule planning: Avoid confrontation entirely | Call in a favor or old debt | Consult omens, divination, or spiritual guidance | Contact or negotiate with a potential ally/faction | Debate moral/ethical/practical costs | Defer to the party's leader | Eliminate a specific threat first | Follow instinct and improvise | Gather more local rumors or intel | Hire local help or muscle | Learn the local terrain | Look for a local contact, fence, informant, or insider | Make final personal preparations | Pivot to a secondary lead | Plan a distraction or diversion | Prepare healing or protective measures | Propose a high-stakes gamble | Push for a direct, immediate strike | Question the entire mission's purpose | Repair, upgrade, enchant, or modify equipment | Rest and recover before acting | Restock and audit supplies | Scrap the plan and start over | Seek an expert, sage, guide, or specialist | Send someone undercover | Set a trap or ambush | Set signals, code words, or fallback points | Share previously withheld knowledge | Split the party | Study maps, charts, myths, or historical records | Train or practice a specific skill/tactic | Use bribery or social leverage | Volunteer for the dangerous part | Volunteer to scout ahead alone | Wait for better timing/weather/specific event | Warn of a potential trap}"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="Rolling them">
      <outline text="In a fight: {combat}. In a conversation: {social}. On the road: {exploration}. Underground: {delving}. Reading a clue: {interpretation}. Around the fire: {downtime}. Around the map: {planning}."/>
      <outline text="Cross them on purpose, which is the zine's own advice. A downtime move in the middle of a fight: while arrows fly, the bard decides to {downtime}. Colour it with a spark word: he does it {disposition}."/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## How to run it">
    <outline text="Read the adventure until a character has to act. If you already know what they would do, just play it. If you do not, write the Obvious, write as much of the Option and the Odd as comes easily, and roll {tripleo}."/>
    <outline text="Stuck for an Option or an Odd? Roll {actionfocus} or {actionmethod} and read the pair through that character's Traits. Stuck on an NPC nobody wrote? Roll {npcsketch}."/>
    <outline text="Leaning a direction? Double-Down: {1d6} and {1d6}, keep your favourite, and on doubles promote the Trait a lane on the sheet."/>
    <outline text="Skipping your system's mechanics? Define three outcomes instead of three actions and roll the same {tripleo}. Track the damage as Traits rather than numbers: a new TEMPORARY row on the sheet."/>
    <outline text="Keep the record in the same file. Points below a scene heading hold the beats, the party above holds the Traits, and the tables hold the sparks, so the play and the log never drift apart."/>
  </outline>
</outline>
</body></opml>
